Tables
Use the shared atlasTable() helper for searchable and static data tables. It applies the Atlas table styling without injecting per-instance CSS:
```{ojs}
import { atlasTable } from "/helpers/atlasTable.ojs"

atlasTable(data, {
  searchable: true,
  zebra: false,
  columns: ["region", "crop", "value"],
  format: {
    value: (value) =&gt; value.toLocaleString(),
  },
})
```
Set searchable: false when a search control is unnecessary. The previous dataTable() and filterableDataTable() names remain available only as compatibility wrappers; new notebooks should import atlasTable directly.

Selectors
The Observable standard library comes with many inputs/selectors pre-included. These should be used when possible, rather than building selectors from scratch or importing them from elsewhere. Documentation on these selectors can be found here with many examples on ObservableHQ. We use a custom CSS to modify the styling of these inputs, these should be automatically applied on quarto render and preview.
When multiple selectors are used together - such as to select multiple admin levels - they should be grouped together in a single horizontal block using an Inputs.form, or other methods where necessary. When multiple selectors are used across a notebook to select the same variable in different places, there should be one master selector that is updated when other selectors are used (using Inputs.bind()). The below example shows how to do this, along with how to use the Inputs.form selector to group selectors together:
Use atlasMultiSelect() for a normal multiple selector. It commits once, when the menu closes, and only if the selection changed while it was open — never per click. That is deliberate and not configurable: a selection of several units would otherwise re-run every dependent query and redraw every map once per checkbox.
For administrative selectors specifically, use adminSelect() / adminMultiSelect() from helpers/adminSelectors.ojs rather than building your own. The selection lives in that module, one cell per level, so every control on the page stays in sync and a section can render only the levels its data reaches — adminMultiSelect([0, 1]) for data that stops at admin 1.
```{ojs}
import { atlasMultiSelect } from "/helpers/enhancedMultiSelect.ojs"

viewof countries = atlasMultiSelect(countryOptions, {
  label: "Countries",
  value: ["KEN", "TZA"],
  format: (iso3) =&gt; countryLabels.get(iso3) ?? iso3,
  enableSelectAll: true,
  searchable: true,
  requireAtLeastOne: false,
  labels: {
    selectAll: "Select all countries",
    deselectAll: "Deselect all countries",
    noneSelected: "No countries selected",
  },
})
```
When maxSelections is finite, the component omits Select All rather than silently selecting only the first items. Deselect All remains available when empty selections are allowed.
The lower-level enhancedMultiSelect() remains available for advanced cases where the native Observable input must be constructed or bound first.


